<%# WHAT THIS CATEGORY HOLDS (two-ledger spec §3, §4). Local: `presenter`
    (CategoryBudgetPresenter).

    ONE CARD WHERE THERE WERE TWO (Task 7). `_budget_card` printed the pool's standing in the row
    vocabulary and `_pool_card`, four inches below it, drew the SAME pool again with a name, a
    progress bar and a noun of its own — "Envelope" above "Savings Pool" about one pool on one
    afternoon, which took three review rounds to converge and could always drift again. The
    category holds the money now, so there is one thing to describe and one card describing it.

    TWO ARMS, AND THE LINE IS `Category#holder?`: a category with a `funded_since` holds its own
    money, and one without it spends AVAILABLE. The old arms were "points at an account" against
    "points at an envelope or a goal", which is the same distinction asked of a layer that no
    longer exists.

    THE GOAL BAR IS DRAWN ON `HoldingCalculator#saving_toward_a_target?`, NOT `Category#savings?`
    (Task 7's ruling — see the presenter's header). A goal the user also refills at a rate is still
    a goal, and it is now classified as one here, on the entry form's impact card and on the
    categories index card alike.

    THE `Standing` ROW IS A DIFFERENT QUESTION AND KEEPS ITS OWN CONDITION (fix round 1, MED-2).
    `HoldingStatus` is schedule-aware: a DATELESS goal reads `saving`, and a goal carrying an
    anchor-dated rule reads `on track` / `behind` / `won't make it`. So this card can head itself
    `Goal` and stand `on track` in the same breath, which is two facts rather than a contradiction —
    what it IS, and how its schedule is going. %>
<div class="bg-white shadow-sm rounded overflow-hidden border border-gray-100 p-6"
     data-holdings-card
     data-holdings-state="<%= presenter.state %>">
  <% if presenter.holding? %>
    <%# THE CATEGORY HOLDS MONEY. The heading names what it is — a goal, or an envelope — from the
        one predicate, so the word and the bar below it cannot describe different things. %>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-4">
      <%= presenter.saving_toward_a_target? ? "Goal" : "Envelope" %>
    </h2>

    <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
      <% if presenter.saving_toward_a_target? %>
        This category is saving toward a figure. It holds its own money from
        <%= presenter.funded_since.strftime("%b %-d, %Y") %> onward — spending before then read
        against what was available — and nothing here is ever swept back at the end of a period.
      <% else %>
        This category holds its own money from
        <%= presenter.funded_since.strftime("%b %-d, %Y") %> onward — spending before then read
        against what was available. What is in it is what there is to spend, and the rules on the
        Budget page are what fill it.
      <% end %>
    </p>

    <dl class="divide-y divide-gray-200 mb-4">
      <div class="py-3 flex justify-between gap-4">
        <dt class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-500">Holding</dt>
        <dd class="text-sm text-gray-900 font-medium <%= "text-status-danger" if presenter.balance.negative? %>"
            data-figure="balance"><%= number_to_currency(presenter.balance) %></dd>
      </div>

      <div class="py-3 flex justify-between gap-4">
        <dt class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-500">Standing</dt>
        <%# THROUGH `shared/_holding_status`, never hand-threaded: the partial takes ONE object and
            reads all four of the row vocabulary's questions off it, which is what stops a caller
            dropping a suffix the screen beside it keeps. `CategoryBudgetPresenter` answers all
            four. %>
        <dd data-holdings-status>
    <%= render "shared/holding_status", row: presenter %>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="py-3 flex justify-between gap-4">
        <dt class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-500">Holding since</dt>
        <dd class="text-sm text-gray-900" data-figure="funded-since">
          <%= presenter.funded_since.strftime("%b %-d, %Y") %>
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <%# THE PROGRESS BAR IS THE GOAL'S ALONE. `progress_percentage` clamps at both ends, so an
        overdrawn goal draws an empty bar rather than the "-30% complete" the pool card used to
        print on this app's own demo data. %>
    <% if presenter.saving_toward_a_target? %>
      <% progress = presenter.progress_percentage %>
      <div class="mb-4" data-goal-progress>
        <div class="h-2.5 bg-gray-200 rounded-full overflow-hidden">
          <div class="h-2.5 rounded-full <%= progress >= 100 ? "bg-status-success" : "bg-brand" %>"
               style="width: <%= progress %>%;"></div>
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-between text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1">
          <span><%= progress %>% complete</span>
          <span>Target: <%= number_to_currency(presenter.target) %></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <%# THE RULES THAT FILL IT, NAMED. The card lists them and links to the page that edits them —
        it offers no editor of its own, because the money a category has to spend is decided by its
        rules and its allocations, both of which live elsewhere. `pool_rule_label` is the same
        reader Home's expanded row uses, so a rule is named one way on both screens. %>
    <% if presenter.rules? %>
      <div class="mb-4" data-holdings-rules>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-500 mb-2">
          <%= pluralize(presenter.rules.size, "rule") %>
        </h3>
        <ul class="divide-y divide-gray-100">
          <% presenter.rules.each do |rule| %>
            <li class="py-2 flex justify-between gap-4 text-sm">
              <span class="text-gray-700 truncate"><%= pool_rule_label(rule) %></span>
              <span class="text-gray-900 font-medium"><%= number_to_currency(rule.amount) %></span>
            </li>
          <% end %>
        </ul>
      </div>
    <% else %>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4" data-figure="no-rules">
        No rule fills this category — its money arrives when you distribute or move some in by hand.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <%= link_to budget_page_path, class: "w-full inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 rounded border border-gray-300 bg-white text-gray-700 text-sm font-medium hover:bg-gray-50 shadow-sm transition", data: { budget_link: true } do %>
      <%= heroicon "adjustments-horizontal", options: { class: "h-4 w-4" } %>
      <span>Rules on the Budget page</span>
    <% end %>
  <% else %>
    <%# THE CATEGORY HOLDS NOTHING, so its spending drains AVAILABLE (§2, §4).

        THE REGISTER IS THE APP'S EXISTING ONE for this exact shape — `entries/_impact`'s honest
        card and `budget_page/_suggestion_rate`'s "currently comes out of what's available", both
        of which speak about the same population (`Category#holder?` rejected). Three screens, one
        sentence.

        NO EDITOR, and the sentence says why rather than leaving a missing button to be noticed:
        nothing reserves this money, and the way to change that is to give the category a rule on
        the Budget page — which is what the pointer below offers. %>
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-4">Available</h2>

    <%# ** THE HONEST ARM FOR A NON-HOLDER THAT STILL HAS MONEY IN IT (final fix wave, I-1, belt). **
        `CategoryBudgetPresenter#stranded?` carries the whole argument: no live path can produce this
        state once the stamp and the clearing refusal are in, and a planted or pre-wave row still
        must not be told $400 of its own money is not there. The figure is printed under the SAME
        `data-figure="balance"` hook the holding arm uses, because it is the same question with the
        same answer — what this category holds — and a second hook would let a screen-level assertion
        pass over a card that shows nothing. %>
    <% if presenter.stranded? %>
      <p class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-900 mb-1" data-figure="headline">
        This category still holds <%= number_to_currency(presenter.balance) %>, but it isn't set up to
        hold money.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
        Money was moved in and then its holding date was removed, so its spending comes out of what's
        available while this sits here doing nothing. Move it back to available, or set a holding
        date to start counting this category's spending against it.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-900 font-medium mb-4" data-figure="balance">
        <%= number_to_currency(presenter.balance) %>
      </p>
    <% else %>
      <p class="text-sm font-medium text-gray-900 mb-1" data-figure="headline">
        This category doesn't hold money yet.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4">
        Its spending comes out of what's available. Nothing reserves money for this category, so
        nothing here can run out — and nothing tells you when it has cost more than you meant it to.
      </p>
    <% end %>

    <%# THE POINTER WHERE THERE IS ONE, AND THE STANDING OFFER WHERE THERE IS NOT — one route to
        /budget either way, never both. This arm IS the rate detector's population, so it is
        precisely the shape the panel is most likely to be proposing a rule for, and the pointer
        names what is waiting. Without a proposal the card would otherwise be a dead end, so it
        makes the offer in the impact card's own words. %>
    <% if presenter.suggested? %>
      <%= render "categories/_partials/show/suggestion_pointer", presenter: presenter %>
    <% else %>
      <%= link_to budget_page_path, class: "inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-medium text-brand hover:text-brand-dark" do %>
        <span>Give it a rule on the Budget page</span>
        <%= heroicon "arrow-right", options: { class: "h-4 w-4" } %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</div>
